Q: Groovy Spock prints stacktrace though exception is caught A: <p>First of all - check what specific exception gets thrown and gets outside the <code>try-catch</code> block. You have to be aware that following code in Groovy:</p>

<pre class="lang-groovy prettyprint-override"><code>try {
   // do something
} catch (e) {
   // do something with exception
}
</code></pre>

<p>is an equivalent of:</p>

<pre class="lang-groovy prettyprint-override"><code>try {
   // do something
} catch (Exception e) {
   // do something with exception
}
</code></pre>

<p>It means that <code>java.lang.Throwable</code> and all its children classes (except <code>java.lang.Exception</code>) are not caught inside your try catch. For instance:</p>

<pre><code>def call() {
    try {
        throw new Error('Something wrong happened')
    } catch (e) {
        false
    }
}
</code></pre>

<p>This exception won't get caught by <code>try-catch</code> and you will see something like this in the console log:</p>

<pre><code>java.lang.Error: Something wrong happened

    at com.github.wololock.micronaut.TestSpec$Service.call(TestSpec.groovy:22)
    at com.github.wololock.micronaut.TestSpec.test(TestSpec.groovy:13)
</code></pre>

<p>It happens, because <code>java.lang.Error</code> extends <code>java.lang.Throwable</code> and it is not a child class of <code>java.lang.Exception</code>.</p>

<p>If you want to catch all possible exceptions that may happened to your code you would have to use <code>Throwable</code> inside the catch block, something like this:</p>

<pre><code>def call() {
    try {
        throw new Error('Something wrong happened')
    } catch (Throwable e) {
        false
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: Accessing postgres db using pgxpool in a goroutine A: <p>Usually, you would create a single Postgres connection pool somewhere early in your app's startup code. Then you pass the connection pool as a dependency to your request handlers. Or to whatever abstractions you are using inside your request handlers, like repositories.</p>
<p>This approach works with the <a href="https://github.com/labstack/echo" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Echo</a> framework just as well.</p>
<p>For example, you could create a request handler like so:</p>
<pre class="lang-golang prettyprint-override"><code>type GreetingHandler struct {
    db *pgxpool.Pool
}

func NewGreetingHandler(db *pgxpool.Pool) *GreetingHandler {
    return &amp;GreetingHandler{db: db}
}

func (h *GreetingHandler) Handle(c echo.Context) error {
    ctx := context.Background()
    greeting := &quot;&quot;
    err := h.db.QueryRow(ctx, &quot;SELECT 'Hello, world!'&quot;).Scan(&amp;greeting)
    if err != nil {
        return c.String(http.StatusInternalServerError, err.Error())
    }
    return c.String(http.StatusOK, greeting)
}
</code></pre>
<p>The <code>GreetingHandler</code> takes a <code>pgxpool.Pool</code> as a dependency. When handling a request in a goroutine, it calls <code>h.db.QueryRow</code>, which internally acquires a connection from the pool and executes the query.</p>
<p>Then you could wire the <code>GreetingHandler</code> to your Postgres connection pool and Echo instance as follows:</p>
<pre class="lang-golang prettyprint-override"><code>func main() {
    ctx := context.TODO()

    // Create a connection pool early before setting up request handlers.
    pool, err := pgxpool.New(ctx, os.Getenv(&quot;DB_URL&quot;))
    if err != nil {
        log.Fatal(err)
    }
    defer pool.Close()

    // Make sure the database is reachable.
    if err := pool.Ping(ctx); err != nil {
        log.Fatal(err)
    }

    // Pass the connection pool as a dependency to the request handler.
    greeting := NewGreetingHandler(pool)

    // Register the request handlers to an Echo server, then start the server.
    e := echo.New()
    e.GET(&quot;/hello&quot;, greeting.Handle)
    if err := e.Start(&quot;:8080&quot;); err != http.ErrServerClosed {
        log.Fatal(err)
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: C++ std::span overload resolution A: <blockquote>
<p>It is easy to change the call to workaround the problem, but since there is no warning, it isn't obvious to the person writing the call what the problem is, or how to fix it.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Compared to GCC, Clang explicitly gives a <a href="https://godbolt.org/z/57Gj1Pzjf" rel="nofollow noreferrer">warning for this</a>:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;source&gt;:15:7: warning: address of array 'a' will always evaluate to 'true' [-Wpointer-bool-conversion]
   15 |     f(a);
      |     ~ ^
</code></pre>
<blockquote>
<p>Is there a way to change the function definition, or add an additional overload, that would be a better match for the C-style array to avoid the conversion to <code>bool</code>?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You can constrain the function to take any range that is converted to a <code>span</code>:</p>
<pre><code>template&lt;class R&gt;
  requires requires (R&amp;&amp; r) { std::span(std::forward&lt;R&gt;(r)); }
extern void f(R&amp;&amp; r)
{
  const auto sp = std::span(std::forward&lt;R&gt;(r));
  // use as span
}
</code></pre>

Q: How do I count part of a string up to a specific character? A: <ul>
<li><p>Extended Pascal as laid out by ISO standard 10206 defines the built‐in function <code>index</code>.
<code>Index(source, pattern)</code> returns the first character’s index of the first occurence of <code>pattern</code> in <code>source</code>, or zero in case <code>pattern</code> does not appear in <code>source</code>.</p>
<p>Because (in a mathematician’s mind) the empty word (ε), the empty string, i. e. the value <code>''</code>, appears everywhere, <code>index(source, '')</code> always returns <code>1</code>.</p>
<p>Except for the behavior with ε, Delphi’s <code>pos(pattern, source)</code> is equivalent to Extended Pascal’s <code>index(source, pattern)</code>,
although mind the swapped parameter order.
<code>Pos</code> is also supported by the FreePascal Compiler.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Furthermore, Extended Pascal defines how to access sub‐strings.
The notation <code>myStringValue[firstCharacterIndex‥firstCharacterIndex+count]</code> yields <code>count</code> characters of <code>myStringValue</code> starting at <code>firstCharacterIndex</code>, provided that both <code>firstCharacterIndex</code> and <code>firstCharacterIndex+count</code> are valid indices in <code>myStringValue</code>, i. e. the character values exist.</p>
<p>This notation is only permissible for <code>string</code> values that are not <code>bindable</code>.
For <code>string</code> values and variables that are or are not <code>bindable</code>, Extended Pascal defines a built‐in function <code>subStr</code>.
An invocation of the form <code>subStr(stringOrCharacterValue, firstCharacter, count)</code> yields <code>count</code> characters of <code>stringOrCharacterValue</code> starting at <code>firstCharacter</code> provided that the indices are valid just like above.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Putting things together:</p>
<pre class="lang-pascal prettyprint-override"><code>program addressBookDemo(input, output);
const
    contactIndexMinimum =   1;
    contactIndexMaximum =   2;
    lineIndexMaximum    =  80;
    separator           = '#';
    { We are looking for a “Donald”. }
    needle              = 'Donald';
type
    contactIndex        = contactIndexMinimum‥contactIndexMaximum;
    lineIndex           = 1‥lineIndexMaximum;
    line                = string(lineIndexMaximum);
var
    contact:              array[contactIndex] of line;
    separatorIndex:       lineIndex;
    currentContactIndex:  contactIndex;
begin
    contact[1] ≔ 'David Serede#dser@someville.town';
    contact[2] ≔ 'Donald Duck#bigd@duckburg.town';

    for currentContactIndex ≔ contactIndexMinimum
                            to contactIndexMaximum do
    begin
        separatorIndex ≔ index(contact[currentContactIndex], separator);

        if index(
                { The range notation (`1‥n`) is inclusive.
                  Therefore `pred(separatorIndex)` excludes the hash. }
                contact[currentContactIndex][1‥pred(separatorIndex)],
                needle
            ) &gt; 0 then
        begin
            { The advantage of `subStr` is that its third parameter is optional.
              If you omit the third parameter, `subStr` returns everything
              until the respective end of the string. }
            writeLn(subStr(contact[currentContactIndex], succ(separatorIndex)))
        end
    end;
end.
</code></pre>
</li>
<li><p>You may notice that the search <em>is case‐sensitive</em>.
Both the search pattern and source string need to match exactly, <em>including</em> the casing of letters.
The common strategy is to perform <em>case folding</em>, i. e. transform both strings to one common casing (e. g. both become lower‐case) before performing any comparison or <code>index</code> search.</p>
<p>For modern English alphabet letters only, this works fairly well.
Delphi and FreePascal provide the <code>lowercase</code> and <code>upcase</code> functions.
You have a problem, though, if you need to support non‐English names as well.
In some cases the mapping between upper‑ and lower‐case letter depends on the (natural) language.</p>
</li>
</ul>
